---
<Base
  title="B. P. Mouat, Baker, 75 Commercial Street"
  description="Basil Pole Mouat's bakery at 75 Commercial Street, Lerwick, c. 1885–1927: a timeline from the Shetland Times and Bayanne."
>
  <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; Lerwick notes</div>

  <h1>B. P. Mouat, Baker, 75 Commercial Street, Lerwick</h1>

  <NoteEssay>
    <figure>
      <img src="/images/notes/bp-mouat-shop.jpg" width="1400" height="1027" alt="Staff outside the shop under the sign 'B. P. Mouat, Baker', No. 75 Commercial Street, Lerwick" />
    </figure>

    <p>Basil Pole Mouat (1855–1929) ran a bakery at 75 Commercial Street, Lerwick, from some time after 1885 until <strong>10 December 1927</strong>. It closed because the bake-shop had been condemned. He had worked for George Laurence, baker and grocer, from his schooldays, and in 1887 married Laurence’s wife’s niece. In 1906 the Shetland Times called it “the oldest established bakery business in Shetland”.</p>

    <p class="note">A side note on a Lerwick business and family, separate from the political history on the rest of this site.</p>

    <p>Unless marked otherwise, what follows comes from the Shetland Times (through the British Newspaper Archive) and from Bayanne.</p>

    <h2>Timeline</h2>
  </NoteEssay>

  <NoteTable headers={['Date', 'What happened', 'Source']} rows={timeline.map((r) => [{ text: r.date, nowrap: true }, { html: r.text }, { sources: r.sources }])} />

  <NoteEssay>
    <h2>The shop and the building</h2>

    <p>The shop was at the south end of Commercial Street. The family lived above and next door, at 69a, and the bakehouse was at No. 69.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>An old house.</strong> In 1891 the paper said <ExternalLink href={bayanne('I17406')} label="Barbara Pitcairn" />, of the Busta story, once lived here, in her mother’s house. It gave the address as <strong>97</strong> Commercial Street, so the street numbers have probably changed since.</li>
      <li><strong>Numbering.</strong> Mouat’s daughter Jessie was born at “73 Commercial Street” in 1887 (Bayanne). The 1927 notices and the photo both say <strong>75</strong>, and a 1932 note says <strong>73</strong>. It looks like one shop under shifting numbers, but that isn’t confirmed.</li>
      <li><strong>Owner.</strong> Dr J. F. Robertson owned the block. Mouat’s shop and Alex. Sutherland’s grocery were on the ground floor, with houses above.</li>
      <li><strong>1907 alterations.</strong> The walls were raised 4 ft, the shop ceilings heightened and the windows enlarged. This may help date the photo.</li>
      <li><strong>After Mouat.</strong> The Thule Bakery repaired and reopened the bakehouse in December 1928. By 1932 Malcolmson &amp; Co., bakers, had the shop.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The business and its people</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Bread for the fleet.</strong> In 1905 he shared the bread order for the visiting Fleet with J. Groat and Black &amp; Son. From 1908 he held the Admiralty contract on his own.</li>
      <li><strong>Outside catering.</strong> From about 1903 to 1924 he was Lerwick’s usual caterer: Territorial and RNR dances and sports, the Up-Helly-Aa halls, the county show marquees, regattas and church parades. The reports kept saying he “purveyed in his usual first-class style”.</li>
      <li><strong>Staff named in the papers.</strong> Mr Johnston was his foreman for many years and died in 1921. <ExternalLink href={bayanne('I107327')} label="Robert S. Garriock" /> baked for him before moving to the Steamers Store, and died in 1945. <ExternalLink href={bayanne('I29457')} label="Gilbert Hunter" /> of Nesting came to the bakery as a lad, under Mrs Laurence, and stayed “for several years”. He later baked in Edinburgh (his own shop in Easter Road), at sea as a steamer steward, at J. Black &amp; Sons, at Voe and at Mid Yell. He was a Thule Bakery partner from 1928, and died at 69 Commercial Street on 8 Nov 1931 (<ExternalLink href={sn('19311112', 4, 50)} label="Shetland News 12 Nov 1931" />). A man who died in 1949 had served his apprenticeship with him before becoming a docker. In 1899 the bakers’ association sent him three long-service cards for his workers. He advertised for lads and message boys between 1902 and 1923.</li>
      <li><strong>The man.</strong> His obituary calls him quiet and unassuming, with “a fund of quiet humour”, taking no active part in public life. He was Steward of Morton Lodge (89) of Freemasons for 35–40 years, and passed through all the offices of the Lord Clyde Lodge of Oddfellows, which he joined in about 1877.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Family</h2>

    <p>The bakery stayed in one extended family. George Laurence’s second wife, Ann Davidson, was the sister of <ExternalLink href={bayanne('I85534')} label="Janet Davidson" />, whose daughter married Mouat.</p>
  </NoteEssay>

  <NoteTable headers={['Person', 'Dates', 'Link to the bakery']} rows={family.map((p) => [{ text: p.name, href: p.href }, { text: p.dates, nowrap: true }, { html: p.link }])} />

  <NoteEssay>
    <p>The obituary of Mrs Laughton (<ExternalLink href={bayanne('I132735')} label="Mary Cheyne Spence Mouat" />), who died at Hokitika, New Zealand, in 1937, calls her a sister of “the late Mr B. P. Mouat, baker and confectioner” (<ExternalLink href={st('19370925', 4, 76)} label="ST 25 Sep 1937" />).</p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>

    <p>The Shetland Times issues are linked in the timeline. Also used:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><ExternalLink href={st('19290504', 5, 107)} label="Obituary, “Mr Basil P. Mouat”" />, Shetland Times, 4 May 1929, p5</li>
      <li><ExternalLink href={st('18911128', 3, 37)} label="Barbara Pitcairn and the house" />, Shetland Times, 28 Nov 1891, p3</li>
      <li><ExternalLink href={st('19320430', 4, 23)} label="1932 note on No. 73" />, Shetland Times, 30 Apr 1932, p4</li>
      <li><ExternalLink href={st('19210716', 4, 81)} label="Mouat’s foreman Johnston" />, Shetland Times, 16 Jul 1921, p4</li>
      <li><ExternalLink href={st('19451019', 2, 53)} label="R. S. Garriock" />, Shetland Times, 19 Oct 1945, p2</li>
      <li>Bayanne (Shetland Family History): the family pages linked above. They also note the 1929 inventory of his estate (SC12/36/19, p107) and two Sheriff Court actions in which he is a “bread and biscuit baker, Commercial Street” (1902, 1918).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The Shetland Times links need a British Newspaper Archive subscription.</p>
  </NoteEssay>
</Base>
